course: dictionary
lesson: 10786
title: Valuable: worth a lot, or very useful
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Keep your valuables safe, and do not leave them in view in a car.» Valuables are things you own that are worth a lot of money, such as jewellery or phones. The sentence tells you what to do with them, so you can work out the meaning from the clues.
explain: Valuable is an adjective, so it can describe a thing: «Rare Ming porcelain can sell for millions at auction.» Its plural noun form is valuables. Try the meaning in the sentence: "The porcelain is worth a lot of money" still makes sense, so valuable fits.
explain: Valuable can also mean very useful or important, even when no money is involved. «Every life, big or small, is valuable and worth caring for.» Here it means important and precious. Ask yourself which meaning fits the sentence: money, or importance?
fact: Valuable means worth a lot of money.
fact: Valuable can also mean very useful or important, as in «valuable advice» or «valuable time».
fact: Valuables are small things you own that are worth a lot, like jewellery or phones.
fact: «Valuable» is an adjective: valuable, more valuable, most valuable.
fact: Money or something valuable found at school should go to a teacher or the office.
exercise: What are valuables? | Old school books that nobody reads | *Small things you own that are worth a lot, like jewellery or phones | Bags of sweets
exercise: «Thank you for your valuable advice.» What does valuable mean here? | Very long | Very noisy | *Very useful or important | Very cheap
exercise: «Money or something valuable found at school should go to a teacher or the office.» What should you do with money or something valuable you find at school? | Spend it at break time | Keep it in your pocket | *Give it to a teacher or the office | Hide it in the bin
test: «The beaver fur was so valuable that the beaver became a symbol of Canada.» What does valuable mean here? | *Worth a lot | Very light | Easy to find | Very wet
test: Which of these would count as valuables? | *A gold ring | An empty crisp packet | A chewed pencil | A torn page
exam: Which sentence uses valuable to mean worth a lot of money? | «Thank you for your valuable advice.» | «Time is valuable on a school day.» | «She showed valuable patience with the little ones.» | *«The Ming plate was valuable, so it sold for a fortune at auction.»
